    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin.jpg" alt="Geoffrey I of Villehardouin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin">Geoffrey I of Villehardouin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Geoffrey_I_of_Villehardouin_(Schlumberger,_1897).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original design: unknown 13th-century artist; Sketch: Gustave Schlumberger (1844–1929), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> was born of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>. After the crusaders sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin">Geoffrey of Villehardouin</a>, nephew of the chronicler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, was driven by storms to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and joined forces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-champlitte">William of Champlitte</a>, a Burgundian knight who had followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In 1205 they conquered the western Peloponnese, defeating a much larger Greek force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-olive-grove-of-koundouros">Olive Grove of Koundouros</a> in Messenia with a small army that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> puts at about 700 men. Champlitte took the title Prince of Achaea, but when he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1208 to claim an inheritance and died on the journey, Geoffrey I of Villehardouin took control, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ravennika">Parliament of Ravennika</a> in 1209 he was recognised as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Latin Emperor Henry</a>. The conquest of the peninsula took four decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-sgouros">Leo Sgouros</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> until his death in 1208, Corinth fell in 1210 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> in 1212, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> kept the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> in the south-west. Geoffrey’s younger son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a> completed the conquest by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> in 1248 after a long siege, and built the fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-magne">Grand Magne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaufort-castle-lebanon">Beaufort</a> to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> and the Slavic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melingoi">Melingoi</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taygetus">Taygetus</a> range.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/glarentza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glarentza.jpg" alt="Glarentza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Glarentza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2022_Church_of_St_Francis_ruin,_Glarentza_(01).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chris06, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> was a society of conquest in which a small Frankish elite ruled a large Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> population. The French knights who settled in the castles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> were few, and they relied on the Greek landowning archons, who kept their estates and status in return for service and loyalty to the prince, while the peasants, the <em>paroikoi</em>, continued to work the land under obligations much like those of Byzantine times. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hierarchy headed by the Archbishop of Patras replaced the Orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and Cistercian, Franciscan and Dominican houses, along with estates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Hospitallers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, were founded, but Orthodox priests kept their parishes and most of the population remained Orthodox. In time intermarriage produced a population of mixed descent, called <em>gasmouloi</em> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. The economy rested on grain, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, livestock and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, much of it exported through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Clarentza</a>, the principality’s main port, where Venetian, Genoese and later Florentine merchants did business. From the mid-thirteenth century a mint at Clarentza struck deniers tournois on the French model, which circulated throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankokratia">Latin Greece</a>. The court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andravida">Andravida</a> was famous for its French culture: the Venetian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marino-sanudo-torsello">Marino Sanudo Torsello</a> wrote that French was spoken there as well as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and tournaments, such as the one held at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a> in 1305, were part of aristocratic life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karydi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-karydi.jpg" alt="Battle of Karydi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karydi">Battle of Karydi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ShepherdByzempire1265.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William R. Shepherd, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The principality’s strength rested on mounted knights who held fiefs in return for military service, on a network of castles and on the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, the Latin Empire and later the Angevin kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Geoffrey I built the great castle of Chlemoutsi, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont</a>, in about 1220–1223, reputedly with church revenues he had seized, and strongholds such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a> and Kalavryta guarded the baronies. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> was the leading military power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankokratia">Latin Greece</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-euboeote-succession">War of the Euboeote Succession</a> he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-de-la-roche">Guy I de la Roche</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karydi">Battle of Karydi</a> in 1258. The following year, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II of Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily">Manfred of Sicily</a> against the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, William was defeated and captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a>. To win his release in 1262 he ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-magne">Grand Magne</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> a foothold from which they waged a long war of reconquest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> won notable victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-prinitza">Prinitza</a> in 1263 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-makryplagi">Makryplagi</a> in 1264, but the frontier moved steadily westward. Later conflicts included the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-of-majorca">Ferdinand of Majorca</a>, who was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manolada">Battle of Manolada</a> in 1316, and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navarrese-company">Navarrese Company</a>, a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> that seized much of the principality around 1380. From the 1390s Ottoman raids devastated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin.jpg" alt="William II of Villehardouin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_II_of_Achaea_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> is often described as a model feudal state. The prince ruled with a council of his great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and prelates, which acted as the High Court, and the land was divided among twelve baronies, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akova">Akova</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a>, Patras, Vostitsa, Kalavryta, Chalandritsa and Passavant, whose lords held lesser fiefs of their own and owed military service. Under the Villehardouins the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, the Duke of the Archipelago and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquisate-of-bodonitsa">Marquis of Bodonitsa</a> recognised the Prince of Achaea as their suzerain. Custom was eventually codified in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assizes-of-romania">Assizes of Romania</a>, compiled in the second quarter of the fourteenth century, which combined French feudal law with Byzantine practice and was later adopted in Venetian territories. Greek archons could hold fiefs and sometimes rose into the nobility, and Byzantine fiscal practices survived at the local level. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> accepted the suzerainty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-viterbo-1267">Treaty of Viterbo</a> in 1267, the principality passed on his death in 1278 to the Angevin kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, who governed through bailiffs and granted it to members of their family or to the husbands of William’s heiress, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-villehardouin">Isabella of Villehardouin</a>. Absentee rule, disputed claims and the growing power of the barons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> captains weakened central authority, until in 1396 the Navarrese captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-san-superano">Pedro de San Superano</a> was recognised as prince.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea.jpg" alt="Chronicle of the Morea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Text_from_the_Chronicle_of_Morea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> was the most enduring and successful of the crusader states created in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> after 1204, and for more than two centuries it brought Western feudal institutions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-language">French language</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> into the heart of the Byzantine world. Its history is preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a>, which survives in Greek, French, Aragonese and Italian versions and is a principal source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankokratia">Frankish Greece</a>; the Greek version, written in vernacular political verse, is also an important work of medieval Greek literature. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assizes-of-romania">Assizes of Romania</a> remained in use in Venetian possessions in Greece long after the principality had vanished. The castles of Chlemoutsi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Glarentza</a> still mark the landscape of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, and the ruined Gothic church of Saint Sophia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andravida">Andravida</a> shows the Western architecture the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> introduced. The principality’s greatest legacy for Greece, however, was unintended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a>, the fortress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> built in 1249 and ceded in 1262, became the capital of the Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a>, a centre of late Byzantine art and learning associated with the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gemistos-plethon">George Gemistos Plethon</a>. The long coexistence of Franks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> also left marks on the law, language and culture of the Morea, and fed later Western images of chivalry in the East.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-hainaut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-hainaut.jpg" alt="Matilda of Hainaut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-hainaut">Matilda of Hainaut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matilda_of_Hainaut_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The principality’s decline began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> and the cession of the fortresses in 1262, which planted a Byzantine province in the south-east of the peninsula. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> advanced steadily, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, having lost the Latin Empire in 1261, depended on the distant Angevin kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. After the Villehardouin line ended with Isabella’s daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-hainaut">Matilda of Hainaut</a>, rival Angevin, Majorcan and Savoyard claimants divided the barons. In 1377 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-i-of-naples">Joanna I of Naples</a> leased the principality to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, who brought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navarrese-company">Navarrese Company</a>, and its captains made themselves masters of much of the country, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-san-superano">Pedro de San Superano</a> as prince from 1396. In 1404 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/centurione-ii-zaccaria">Centurione II Zaccaria</a>, a Genoese baron of Chalandritsa and nephew of Pedro’s widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-ii-zaccaria">Maria Zaccaria</a>, became prince. Squeezed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tocco-family">Tocco</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and Ottoman raiders, he lost ground steadily. In 1428 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine Palaiologos</a>, the future last Byzantine emperor, gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Glarentza</a> by marrying a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco">Carlo I Tocco</a>, and in 1429–1430 he took Patras from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> archbishop. In 1430 Centurione agreed to marry his daughter Catherine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-palaiologos">Thomas Palaiologos</a>, with the principality as her dowry, keeping only his castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyparissia">Kyparissia</a>) in Messenia. On his death in 1432 Thomas annexed the remainder, ending Frankish rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> outside the Venetian ports; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered the Despotate in 1460.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, founded by French knights after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, was the most durable crusader state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, a model feudal society whose court rivalled those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and its loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a> in 1262 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> the base from which it reconquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> by 1432.</em></p>

